WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.816
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.410
AL HAMEL: Welcome to CMP 302.

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:11.610
We're going to do some
benchmarking of GCE

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:17.040
on this-- well, GCP, but mostly
GCE since this session is

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.705
Dory-enabled.

00:00:18.705 --> 00:00:20.330
Please save your
questions for the end,

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:21.660
and please use the Dory.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:25.530
And it'll be moderated
after the session.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.560
So what's in store for you?

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.780
We'll be talking first
about Perfkitbenchmarker.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:33.650
Perfkitbenchmarker is an
open source project at Google

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:36.380
where we're trying to
make a set of tools

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:39.290
to make benchmarking
easy for everyone,

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:42.018
trying to figure out which
cloud provider to use.

00:00:42.018 --> 00:00:44.060
Then we're going to go
over a slew of benchmarks,

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:45.410
probably too many data slides.

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:47.000
I don't know how I
got them approved.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.630
But there's a vast
number of slides

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:52.520
that will show IAS
benchmarks in many ways.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.340
Then last week, we
announced the C2.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:58.660
It's an instance type
at Google for a GCE.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:01.480
And I'll be comparing that
to our older N1 instance,

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.530
and we'll see if
performance is better

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:06.080
or if it's different on
the C2 versus the N1.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.450
Spoilers-- slightly better.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:10.330
And then I'll be
giving you a couple

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:12.560
of demos and some
scripts to take

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.950
home that are pretty easy to
use if you want to compare

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:18.720
and contrast different
instances, different-- almost

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:19.220
anything.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:23.680
You want to slice and
dice IAS pretty easily.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:24.810
OK.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:26.250
What is PKB?

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:30.240
Like I said, it's an
open source set of tools

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.860
that we've created
to try and make

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.500
it easy to compare
the performance

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.300
of various components
of the cloud platforms.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:40.760
It supports most
cloud providers.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:44.150
It has on the order
of 50 different tests

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:46.910
that you can run to
try and get performance

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:50.640
data on the different providers
or the different services.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.850
I think it's particularly
good at getting IAS.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:58.310
We've started working with GCE
pretty early on this project,

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:01.818
and we think we hit
most of the major touch

00:02:01.818 --> 00:02:03.860
points that people are
interested in with respect

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.350
to performance for GCE.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.905
We also have a number
of tests for ML

00:02:08.905 --> 00:02:10.280
which are being
added constantly.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.450
I put more resources over
time into our ML tests.

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:17.450
They test all the different
configurations via GPU,

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:22.560
TPU, or just running on
the processor itself.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.890
We also do database tests, some.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:28.430
Database testing is
a big art, and we

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:31.770
are continuing expanding our
set of tests for databases.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:33.720
But it's not complete
at this point.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.830
We need-- it's a vast amount of
work to get them all complete,

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:38.390
but we're working on it.

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:40.230
And then we have
some big data tests,

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:44.030
so some tests that hit
BigQuery, Dataproc,

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.400
Dataflow, and a bunch
of other services,

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.380
and compare and contrast them.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:51.040
But these services are a
little bit bespoke sometimes,

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.040
so comparing them can be
a little bit difficult.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.100
But we give it a shot.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:57.020
And please try it and
tell us what you think.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:58.700
Some random stats about PKB--

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:00.830
it's a moderately
successful project.

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:02.900
We have a little
bit over 300 forks.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:06.380
We have 1.5k watch star forks.

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:10.720
We get on the order of 3.7k
commits, so 4,000 commits,

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:16.260
and about 100 contributors
across a number of companies.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.010
What I really get a
kick out of, though,

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:21.520
is quite often when I see
an industry report come out

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:23.470
from one of the
big vendors, and I

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:25.740
look at the addendum
or the appendix,

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:29.610
and I can see how they did
it, it's pretty common now

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.800
that I'm seeing PKB
referenced and used

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:34.700
as scripts to generate
their reports, which is--

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:38.430
it's starting to get more and
more use for benchmarking,

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:40.630
especially, like I said, IAS--

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:44.130
IAS being Infrastructure
As a Service-- so VMs,

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:46.060
networking, disk.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:48.720
We'll go over those.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.240
So why do you want to
benchmark the cloud?

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:56.390
Well, performance is usually
one of the three top metrics

00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:59.180
that people use when trying
to decide what cloud go to.

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:00.860
Security obviously top of mind.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:02.330
It's been in every keynote.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:04.700
Very, very important.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:07.967
Reliability, availability
very much top of mind.

00:04:07.967 --> 00:04:09.800
Benchmarking-- that is
moderately difficult.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:12.680
We'll talk about
that near the end.

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:14.160
But that's also top of mind.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.200
But performance, or price
performance, quite commonly,

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.089
is right up there in
purchase decisions,

00:04:20.089 --> 00:04:23.370
so it's moderately important.

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:25.130
The best way to get
performance data

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:28.550
is by doing POCs, of course.

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:31.855
So you take all of
your apps, you somehow

00:04:31.855 --> 00:04:33.230
come up with some
model where you

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:34.850
can bundle the good
percentage of them

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:36.860
up with the crucial
parts that you believe.

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:39.110
You move them up to the
cloud, and you run that.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.810
The problem with POCs is
that they're quite expensive,

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:44.760
and they're very-- they're
just complicated/expensive.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.572
So they're the
best but expensive.

00:04:46.572 --> 00:04:48.530
Is there something you
can do beforehand, maybe

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:50.330
kick the tires, that's cheaper?

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:51.320
Yes.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:52.640
Especially, like I said, IAS--

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.290
the models are pretty simple
for figuring out how IAS works.

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:55.790
I guess.

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:59.000
IAS is unbelievably complicated
underneath the hood.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.710
But from a user's
perspective, when

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:02.543
you think about performance
characteristics,

00:05:02.543 --> 00:05:04.065
it's not overly complicated.

00:05:04.065 --> 00:05:05.690
So we can come up
with a bunch of tests

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:08.180
that allow people to compare.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:10.580
And we'll see a bunch of this.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:14.430
And it's moderately
easy and inexpensive.

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:18.800
So we're going to be hitting
five main metrics on IAS.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.550
Boot time-- it's a weird one.

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:22.800
I know at first you're going
to wonder why boot time.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:25.940
I'll go into why boot
time is important.

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:27.770
After that we'll
talk about compute.

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:32.360
VMs are the dominant expense
for almost every person

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:33.320
who uses cloud.

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:35.780
And what you do on VMs
is generally compute.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:38.960
That's probably, in many ways,
the most important metric.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:41.330
Memory-- a little bit
of a fringe metric,

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:43.333
but it can show you
sometimes some interesting

00:05:43.333 --> 00:05:44.000
characteristics.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.040
And again, incredibly
easy to run

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:47.790
and moderately
easy to interpret,

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:50.220
so might as well kick the tires.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:54.090
Networking-- the VMs are
in the cloud networking.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:57.060
Sending bytes, receiving
bytes is what they do.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:58.830
Incredibly important.

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:00.990
Depending upon the app,
it could be crucial.

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:03.790
Many compute apps are
actually latency bound.

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:06.145
And if you do a lot
of HPC in the cloud--

00:06:06.145 --> 00:06:08.520
there's a number of reasons
why networking is important--

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:10.000
serving.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:14.153
So incredibly important, and
actually shockingly complex.

00:06:14.153 --> 00:06:15.570
From our perspective,
again, we're

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:16.740
just going to do
performance, but there's

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:18.210
so many ways to
slice it, as we'll

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:22.110
see as I inundate you
with slides and data,

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:25.540
that there's a lot to compare
when it comes to networking.

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:28.870
And finally, remote
block devices or disks--

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:30.263
you need to boot your machines.

00:06:30.263 --> 00:06:31.930
You've got to fetch
data from somewhere.

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:33.880
It's either from
usually cloud storage

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:35.530
or from your block device.

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:37.090
Pretty important
in some scenarios,

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:39.670
especially databases
where databases

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:43.720
hammer the remote block
device incredibly hard.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:46.030
And knowing how the
block device responds

00:06:46.030 --> 00:06:48.340
under this kind of hammering
for databases especially,

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:50.770
but all kinds of
solutions, be it

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:53.740
NoSQL databases or
traditional databases,

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:57.190
remote disks can be right
front and center with respect

00:06:57.190 --> 00:06:59.140
to how your overall
app performs.

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:03.790
So benchmarking remote
disks is pretty important.

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:04.300
All right.

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:07.000
Boot times-- like I said, kind
of a weird way to start with.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:10.260
You think compute,
so why boot times?

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:13.950
Well, the first thing is the
customer's first experience

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:15.750
when they get onto the cloud.

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:19.960
I have a really quick story
about this that I find funny.

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:23.640
A friend of mine
has an ML startup.

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:25.050
He's doing moderately well.

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:26.850
He's on some cloud provider.

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:28.800
And another cloud
provider came in

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:31.470
and gave him sufficient
credits to run all of his apps

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:33.240
for two years.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:37.180
So as CEO and the guy who is
responsible for the finances,

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:38.670
he was totally bought in.

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:41.710
And he told his devs, we're
moving to this other cloud.

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:46.210
The other cloud had not the
best boot times in particular,

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:46.780
actually.

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:48.280
There were a number
of other issues,

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:50.650
but boot times really
irritated the devs.

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:52.690
It irritated them so
much that somehow--

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:55.148
and I still don't know how this
is possible-- they rebelled

00:07:55.148 --> 00:07:57.750
against the CEO, and they
stayed with their other provider

00:07:57.750 --> 00:07:58.958
where they were paying money.

00:07:58.958 --> 00:08:01.090
To this day, both he and
I are incredibly confused

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:02.277
how this all played out.

00:08:02.277 --> 00:08:04.360
But it's an amazing story
as to why boot times are

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:07.590
moderately important.

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:09.660
As VM lifetimes shorten--

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:13.440
just rationally, as
VM lifetimes shorten,

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:15.485
the boot time becomes
more and more important.

00:08:15.485 --> 00:08:16.860
And I'm going to
show some data--

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:20.250
both how, internally, when we
view all of your workloads,

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:23.580
how long you keep
machines up, and then

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:26.318
we'll show how internally, for
us, how long we keep machines

00:08:26.318 --> 00:08:28.110
up, and then aspirationally,
where we think

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:29.877
we should go on that.

00:08:29.877 --> 00:08:31.710
And finally, it really
does show-- boot time

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:33.280
shows technical excellence.

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:34.860
They hit the whole stack.

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:37.049
You hit the top of
the control plane.

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:38.970
You hit the job scheduling.

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:42.289
You hit network programming,
which is crazy complicated,

00:08:42.289 --> 00:08:45.508
PD programming, and you
hit the actual data plane

00:08:45.508 --> 00:08:47.550
to get the bytes onto the
machine and the machine

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:48.620
to get booted.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:50.623
And if you can drive
that time down,

00:08:50.623 --> 00:08:53.040
it shows technical excellence
throughout the entire stack.

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:55.960
I think it's a really
important metric.

00:08:55.960 --> 00:08:58.920
So then I'll show you great
crazy graphs of where our boot

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:01.140
times are today, and
then I'll show you

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.360
if you can measure
this yourself,

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:06.560
assuming my laptop
keeps powered.

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:09.920
Lifespan of VMs-- so
today, this is what

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:14.250
you guys do in our cloud today.

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:15.050
This is a CDF.

00:09:15.050 --> 00:09:16.467
I should tell you,
actually-- this

00:09:16.467 --> 00:09:19.010
is my favorite-- you're going
to see way too many CDFs today.

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:22.130
A CDF shows, at a
given percentile,

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:24.330
what the value of interest is.

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:26.100
So in this case, on
the vertical axis,

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:29.510
it shows you the percentile,
be it 0% to 100%.

00:09:29.510 --> 00:09:31.580
And along the
horizontal axis, that

00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:34.970
is hours of duration of the VM.

00:09:34.970 --> 00:09:37.130
You can see it had a
pretty sharp slope.

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:41.420
And your P50 for all of your
workloads is 12 minutes.

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:46.310
That is the median time of
a VM's life today in GCE.

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:48.770
Pretty short, you would think.

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:50.120
It is pretty short.

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:52.230
And you're doing it
not wrong at all.

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:53.990
I think you're doing
it exactly right.

00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:56.000
The entire idea
behind moving to cloud

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:58.970
is that it's incredibly
responsive and agile.

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:00.320
And you can just use--

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:03.210
you can use compute
the second you need it,

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:05.750
and then you discard it, and
it doesn't cost you anything

00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:06.500
after that.

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:08.083
This is the exact
right way to use it,

00:10:08.083 --> 00:10:10.277
and you're using it right.

00:10:10.277 --> 00:10:12.110
And this is what we do
in Google internally.

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:14.630
In Google internally, we're
doing the same thing you are.

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:17.952
And that's when we need compute,
we spin a container up for us.

00:10:17.952 --> 00:10:19.910
But we spin up a container,
and then as soon as

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:21.410
that compute is on a
server, we throw it away so

00:10:21.410 --> 00:10:22.690
someone else can use it.

00:10:22.690 --> 00:10:25.070
And we're down to
six minutes here.

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:29.210
And we actually think that
aspirationally in GCE, we

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:31.280
should drive this
time down even more.

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:33.450
We love to think of--
we think aspirationally

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:37.530
that VM should be thought of
like threats in that you should

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:39.740
be able to spin up a
VM in a sub-second.

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:41.060
That's the whole stack.

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:43.763
And then we throw it away as
soon as you don't need it.

00:10:43.763 --> 00:10:44.930
We think this is the future.

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:46.370
We're driving towards it.

00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:48.340
But like I said, this
is a full stack problem.

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:49.940
It's moderately hard.

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:51.440
Here's why it's a
full stack problem

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:52.670
and it's moderately hard.

00:10:52.670 --> 00:10:54.850
This is from our
internal monitoring.

00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:56.840
This shows at a high level--

00:10:56.840 --> 00:11:00.370
each one of these lines
shows a large section of code

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:03.520
that is responsible during
a VM boot up bring up.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:05.830
And if I click on this in
our internal monitoring,

00:11:05.830 --> 00:11:09.100
these expand infinitely into
even more subsections that

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:10.500
show-- that are broken down.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:12.070
But this is the high level view.

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:14.170
And what contributes
to boot times?

00:11:14.170 --> 00:11:15.670
It's, of course,
the control plane.

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:18.440
The control plane itself
is incredibly complicated.

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:20.560
It has to do a ton
of business logic.

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:21.790
It has to then do--

00:11:21.790 --> 00:11:23.950
in a really weird number
of quota management.

00:11:23.950 --> 00:11:27.910
It is strange how many
different quotas are modified

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:30.700
in the construction of a VM.

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:32.980
Then that is sent off to
the actual JobScheduler.

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:33.670
Whoa.

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:34.890
I'm on my demo.

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:35.530
A little early.

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:37.000
Sorry.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:39.610
Then it goes on to
the JobScheduler.

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:43.370
And the JobScheduler will find
a place, a physical place,

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:43.900
for that VM.

00:11:43.900 --> 00:11:46.810
We have, as you know,
custom VM types.

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:49.180
This makes it so we have
a bin packing problem

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:52.810
to solve globally
for the cluster.

00:11:52.810 --> 00:11:56.020
Every time you schedule a VM,
we batch it up a little bit

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:59.170
to make it so it isn't
terribly compute intensive.

00:11:59.170 --> 00:12:01.060
So we have to find a
physical place for it.

00:12:01.060 --> 00:12:05.500
Then once it finds the node, a
lot of containers and cgroups

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:06.250
have to be set up.

00:12:06.250 --> 00:12:08.708
There's a lot of computation
that has to happen on the node

00:12:08.708 --> 00:12:09.552
to get it ready.

00:12:09.552 --> 00:12:12.010
Then in the background, we
start programming the incredibly

00:12:12.010 --> 00:12:15.342
complicated virtual networking
while we get the bits onto

00:12:15.342 --> 00:12:17.050
the machine as quickly
as possible from--

00:12:17.050 --> 00:12:20.110
and we program the remote disk,
and we get the machines onto--

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:22.360
the bits onto the
machine to boot the OS

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:23.320
and run through that.

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:25.030
And then there's a little
bit of overhead for SSH

00:12:25.030 --> 00:12:27.350
while we play around with keys
to make it easy for everyone.

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:29.140
But anyway, the total
sum is on the order

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:32.080
of 20 seconds as a P50.

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:34.490
But is there a distribution?

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:35.470
Yes.

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:36.940
Yes, there is a distribution.

00:12:36.940 --> 00:12:40.180
These are our four
main geographies.

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:41.840
I'll talk about
that in a second.

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:43.060
You have EU.

00:12:43.060 --> 00:12:45.070
You have the US.

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:49.480
You have Asia on here,
each one on a series--

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:49.980
on a CDF.

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:53.330
Again, the CDF
percentile vertical.

00:12:53.330 --> 00:12:55.540
Numbering question,
in this term,

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:58.720
is seconds to boot
on the horizontal.

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:00.970
And as you can see, the P50--

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:04.270
if you have your glasses
on-- it's really small.

00:13:04.270 --> 00:13:09.350
The P50 is on the order of 20
seconds for both EU and Asia.

00:13:09.350 --> 00:13:12.350
And it's a little bit
more, maybe 23 for US.

00:13:12.350 --> 00:13:14.240
US is where it
curves a little bit.

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:17.660
That's due to our US zones
being quite a bit larger.

00:13:17.660 --> 00:13:20.960
And there's a constant struggle
between the people making

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:23.840
optimizations throughout
that stack and the problems

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:26.748
they get as these cells
get larger and larger.

00:13:26.748 --> 00:13:28.040
So there's a constant struggle.

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:32.450
This CDF moves back and forth
continually as the poor devs

00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:34.280
struggle mightily.

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:35.060
There is one more.

00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:36.310
You'll see there's four lines.

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:38.180
This is an in-house
joke, I'm sure,

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:41.510
where my staff puts Canada
as one of the top GOs

00:13:41.510 --> 00:13:42.560
because I'm Canadian.

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:43.490
I am positive.

00:13:43.490 --> 00:13:46.320
And they continually
do this to me.

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:46.830
All right.

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:47.850
Let's try a demo.

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:49.637
It's pretty quick, hopefully.

00:14:05.050 --> 00:14:08.290
So what this is doing
is this is running PKB.

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:10.780
We're launching on
the order of 20 VMs

00:14:10.780 --> 00:14:13.700
simultaneously out
there into the cloud.

00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:15.700
These are N1 standard 4s.

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:19.735
And wait-- I'll get
the results page here.

00:14:19.735 --> 00:14:21.110
This is from the
previous result.

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:25.370
It will hopefully change
as PKB does its thing.

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:28.090
So we're going to fan
out 20 machines, only 20.

00:14:28.090 --> 00:14:28.930
You could do 100.

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:29.720
It's up to you.

00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:31.760
But in this case,
we do 20 machines.

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:33.710
We'll record the times.

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:36.460
Some scripts will run to
create a CDF out of this,

00:14:36.460 --> 00:14:40.815
and we will see the CDF within
three minutes, approximately.

00:14:40.815 --> 00:14:42.440
And most of that time
is spent cleanup.

00:14:42.440 --> 00:14:45.910
There's, like, 1.5 minutes
to send out all the machines

00:14:45.910 --> 00:14:47.530
and to gather all the data.

00:14:47.530 --> 00:14:51.490
And then right now, it
is app get updating.

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:52.302
Oh, it's finished.

00:14:52.302 --> 00:14:54.010
It's generating the
graphic at this time.

00:14:54.010 --> 00:14:55.343
And now it's going to tear down.

00:14:55.343 --> 00:14:57.730
We should have made the teardown
asynchronous and hidden,

00:14:57.730 --> 00:15:01.400
but this is what happens
when we make demos.

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:03.940
So we're going to
span out 20 machines

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:07.990
and see if this makes the
previous internal metrics

00:15:07.990 --> 00:15:10.930
that we have for how
fast our boot time is.

00:15:10.930 --> 00:15:16.360
And hopefully it should finish
tearing down momentarily.

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:20.770
Like I said, PKB is a
moderately large project

00:15:20.770 --> 00:15:24.550
that allows us-- this is a
pretty easy script to run.

00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:27.730
The graphics-- if
you're really good at R,

00:15:27.730 --> 00:15:29.042
it's easy to make the graphics.

00:15:29.042 --> 00:15:30.750
That's probably the
hardest part of this.

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:33.150
There are a number
of graphing solutions

00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:34.150
if you want to use that.

00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:36.067
In this case, like I
said, I want it to be all

00:15:36.067 --> 00:15:40.650
self-contained, so we
used R. So anyway--

00:15:40.650 --> 00:15:42.450
oh, of course it's already done.

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:44.045
So if I reload this--

00:15:44.045 --> 00:15:47.320
oh, god.

00:15:47.320 --> 00:15:50.460
All right, that didn't work out.

00:15:50.460 --> 00:15:51.500
Let's go here.

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:54.790
There should be a link
that will be updated.

00:15:54.790 --> 00:15:55.290
Oh, my.

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:00.150
There you go.

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:01.540
So this is the current CDF.

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:04.260
This is the CDF we just
generated of boot times.

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:08.710
You'll see at P50, amazingly,
it was at 15 seconds.

00:16:08.710 --> 00:16:10.020
That's pretty good for us.

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:13.890
This is running Debian
9, which is our best OS.

00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:16.570
Of course, boot time
varies on an OS basis,

00:16:16.570 --> 00:16:17.680
but this is our best.

00:16:17.680 --> 00:16:18.930
All right, back to the slides.

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:26.195
All righty, so compute--

00:16:26.195 --> 00:16:28.070
well, this is what you
do with VMs generally,

00:16:28.070 --> 00:16:30.810
so incredibly important.

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:32.240
Varies on a couple
of dimensions.

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:33.680
Varies on SKU.

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:37.915
We're going to try N1 versus
C2, see if the C2 is any better.

00:16:37.915 --> 00:16:40.040
There's also single-threaded
versus multi-threaded.

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:44.660
This corresponds to SpecInt
versus SpecInt rate.

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:47.690
SpecInt rate is almost
certainly, in almost all cases,

00:16:47.690 --> 00:16:48.540
much more important.

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:51.110
But there are a few fringe
cases where SpecInt speed

00:16:51.110 --> 00:16:51.860
is more important.

00:16:51.860 --> 00:16:55.275
Also, the C2 is even
better at single-threaded,

00:16:55.275 --> 00:16:56.900
so I'm going to bring
it up, of course.

00:16:56.900 --> 00:16:59.840
I can try to sell my C2s.

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:02.250
And then we're going to talk
about can we do this easier.

00:17:02.250 --> 00:17:03.140
We can run SpecInt.

00:17:03.140 --> 00:17:04.220
It's the granddaddy.

00:17:04.220 --> 00:17:05.339
It's the best.

00:17:05.339 --> 00:17:08.630
It's the best metric
overall for doing compute.

00:17:08.630 --> 00:17:09.650
But it takes a while.

00:17:09.650 --> 00:17:11.480
It's moderately
expensive to run.

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:14.450
Can we get something
like SpecInt results

00:17:14.450 --> 00:17:16.395
with some other tool set?

00:17:16.395 --> 00:17:17.770
And we'll see if
that's possible.

00:17:17.770 --> 00:17:19.853
And then we'll see if we
can measure it ourselves.

00:17:19.853 --> 00:17:22.180
I'm not sure I'm going
to run a demo, though.

00:17:22.180 --> 00:17:24.280
So it's a little
bit faint, sadly.

00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:25.270
I might have to--

00:17:25.270 --> 00:17:27.369
I don't know if you can
all see the light green.

00:17:27.369 --> 00:17:29.290
There's two lines
here on the CDF.

00:17:29.290 --> 00:17:31.630
There is the light
green, which is the N1,

00:17:31.630 --> 00:17:34.608
and there is the dark
green, which is the C2.

00:17:34.608 --> 00:17:36.900
The C2, spec and speed, the
single-threaded performance

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:40.930
is in this metric on 8 core VMs.

00:17:40.930 --> 00:17:42.850
Again, this is not
perfect because we

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:46.840
are in a zone by ourself
while the N1s are living

00:17:46.840 --> 00:17:49.360
with a lot of other
people or other customers,

00:17:49.360 --> 00:17:51.550
so they're slightly
disadvantaged.

00:17:51.550 --> 00:17:54.070
We don't know if this result
will hold once we get to prod.

00:17:54.070 --> 00:17:56.290
But right now on single
thread performance,

00:17:56.290 --> 00:17:58.450
we're seeing up
to 60% difference

00:17:58.450 --> 00:18:01.750
between the N1 and the C2,
which is pretty impressive.

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:04.360
You'll note the CDF for the
C2 actually has a little tail

00:18:04.360 --> 00:18:05.950
at the bottom there.

00:18:05.950 --> 00:18:06.760
That's not perfect.

00:18:06.760 --> 00:18:08.170
It should be perfectly vertical.

00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:11.500
We think what happened
here is that I asked

00:18:11.500 --> 00:18:13.510
for a lot of samples
from my staff

00:18:13.510 --> 00:18:17.020
when we cranked it up to 10
in the little, tiny zone we

00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:19.060
had because it's pre-release.

00:18:19.060 --> 00:18:22.600
And we think we were making
multiple versions of SpecInt

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:24.010
hit the same socket.

00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:26.140
And SpecInt at 8
cores pretty much

00:18:26.140 --> 00:18:28.640
saturates the memory
bandwidth of a socket.

00:18:28.640 --> 00:18:31.950
So when you get multiple, they
will interfere with each other.

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:36.720
It's a known issue with
SpecInt and normal CPUs.

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:38.110
You run out of memory bandwidth.

00:18:38.110 --> 00:18:39.910
It's moderately hungry.

00:18:39.910 --> 00:18:41.740
So that 60% lets
you SpecIntRate.

00:18:41.740 --> 00:18:44.350
SpecIntRate is, like I
said, much more important

00:18:44.350 --> 00:18:46.890
than SpecInt speed
for most uses.

00:18:46.890 --> 00:18:48.940
SpecIntRate is multi-threaded.

00:18:48.940 --> 00:18:51.610
What is the
throughput of your VM?

00:18:51.610 --> 00:18:54.140
These are in N1 standard 8's.

00:18:54.140 --> 00:18:56.630
The CDF, again, for--

00:18:56.630 --> 00:18:57.500
there's two lines.

00:18:57.500 --> 00:18:59.833
I don't know if you can see
it all the way in the back--

00:18:59.833 --> 00:19:02.320
one all the way to the right,
one closer to the left.

00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:04.750
Again, CDF with
percentiles and numbering

00:19:04.750 --> 00:19:07.860
question at the bottom.

00:19:07.860 --> 00:19:11.310
And the C2 is doing great.

00:19:11.310 --> 00:19:14.760
On this case with N1 standard
8's or C2 standard 8's, we're

00:19:14.760 --> 00:19:17.910
seeing up to 50%
difference at P50.

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:20.650
During our press release, we
said 40%, which is very safe,

00:19:20.650 --> 00:19:21.150
we think.

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:23.910
We are pretty safe to say
that most of the time,

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:27.660
you will get 40%, at least
40% more performance with a C2

00:19:27.660 --> 00:19:29.900
on a per-core basis than
you will with an N2--

00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:31.650
with an N1, rather.

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:35.030
So it's a pretty
big jump for us.

00:19:35.030 --> 00:19:39.100
I guess I should give a little
bit of data dump on the C2

00:19:39.100 --> 00:19:40.050
itself.

00:19:40.050 --> 00:19:43.753
It's a Cascade Lake
processor running with--

00:19:43.753 --> 00:19:45.420
I don't know if I
should say exactly how

00:19:45.420 --> 00:19:50.190
many cores, but substantially
less than we did inside

00:19:50.190 --> 00:19:51.120
of the N1.

00:19:51.120 --> 00:19:55.395
So therefore, when you go
to NCC versus XEC with Intel

00:19:55.395 --> 00:19:58.020
or with any [INAUDIBLE],, having
less cores on a per-chip means

00:19:58.020 --> 00:20:00.360
you have way more
TDP per core, and you

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:04.090
will get far higher
clock rates and we do.

00:20:04.090 --> 00:20:05.760
Furthermore, we did
a few other things.

00:20:05.760 --> 00:20:08.490
The software is slightly
different in the C2,

00:20:08.490 --> 00:20:13.710
and the memory is clocked
slightly higher in the C2

00:20:13.710 --> 00:20:15.060
than it was in the N1.

00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:17.820
And the overall effect is
a tremendous improvement

00:20:17.820 --> 00:20:21.830
in performance on
a per-core basis.

00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:22.670
All right.

00:20:22.670 --> 00:20:25.938
So SpecInt-- like I said,
SpecInt is the granddaddy.

00:20:25.938 --> 00:20:27.230
I'm not going to fight SpecInt.

00:20:27.230 --> 00:20:29.930
It's, like, 100 PhDs
worked on this for 20 years

00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:32.270
to come up with some
random set of benchmarks

00:20:32.270 --> 00:20:35.480
with some weighting, and
that is the best, still

00:20:35.480 --> 00:20:36.230
the gold standard.

00:20:36.230 --> 00:20:37.730
I will always run it.

00:20:37.730 --> 00:20:41.630
But is there anything we
can do that could perhaps

00:20:41.630 --> 00:20:44.360
be a little bit
cheaper than SpecInt?

00:20:44.360 --> 00:20:46.910
Furthermore, SpecInt takes
a couple hours to run--

00:20:46.910 --> 00:20:50.810
2, 4, sometimes 24 hours to
run depending on VM size.

00:20:50.810 --> 00:20:52.490
And I got a plane to catch.

00:20:52.490 --> 00:20:54.050
I can't wait for 24 hours.

00:20:54.050 --> 00:20:56.003
I can't do that
with you right here.

00:20:56.003 --> 00:20:57.170
We could fork all the tests.

00:20:57.170 --> 00:20:59.450
Obviously, SpecInt
is, as you know,

00:20:59.450 --> 00:21:02.653
comprised of on the order
of 20 tests, 20 to 30 tests.

00:21:02.653 --> 00:21:04.070
We can fork them
all individually,

00:21:04.070 --> 00:21:06.680
bring them all back
in, geomean those,

00:21:06.680 --> 00:21:07.790
and get a number back out.

00:21:07.790 --> 00:21:11.070
But even then, it takes
quite a while to run.

00:21:11.070 --> 00:21:12.360
So what about this?

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:14.330
Why don't we try
running stress-ng?

00:21:14.330 --> 00:21:17.480
It's a package out there
which has a variety--

00:21:17.480 --> 00:21:19.500
maybe 50 different
tests internally.

00:21:19.500 --> 00:21:23.060
Geomean all of those together
and see what the result is.

00:21:23.060 --> 00:21:25.880
It turns out it's pretty
correlated with SpecInt.

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:27.380
There will be-- in
the GitHub, we'll

00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:30.560
give an approximate
correlation coefficient

00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:33.620
to see how good stress-ng
is, but it's pretty cheap.

00:21:33.620 --> 00:21:37.140
So then we'll take
a look at that.

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:38.890
All right, so let's
see if we can do that.

00:21:38.890 --> 00:21:39.500
Let's go to the demo.

00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:41.750
But I don't think I'm going
to make you go all the way

00:21:41.750 --> 00:21:43.670
through a running of it.

00:21:43.670 --> 00:21:45.140
Oh, dear.

00:21:45.140 --> 00:21:45.828
And here we go.

00:21:45.828 --> 00:21:47.120
This is what the end result is.

00:21:47.120 --> 00:21:48.140
I could have hit the button.

00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:50.640
If we have time at the end,
I'll hit the button for everyone

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:53.090
so we can the lines slightly
change from left to right.

00:21:53.090 --> 00:21:57.420
But we got about 55% difference
in performance between the C2--

00:21:57.420 --> 00:21:59.720
or is it 45 in this case--

00:21:59.720 --> 00:22:03.650
between the C2 and the N1, which
is within our bounds at P50.

00:22:03.650 --> 00:22:05.610
And the CDF looks beautiful.

00:22:05.610 --> 00:22:08.030
I love the straight,
vertical CDF.

00:22:08.030 --> 00:22:10.880
That's what you want to see in
a CDF-- incredible consistency.

00:22:10.880 --> 00:22:13.130
And you're getting a little
more consistency on the C2

00:22:13.130 --> 00:22:15.770
that we're running on right
away than we do on the end ones.

00:22:15.770 --> 00:22:17.187
And again, that's
almost certainly

00:22:17.187 --> 00:22:20.237
due to a couple of factors.

00:22:20.237 --> 00:22:22.070
A little bit different
software in the C2 as

00:22:22.070 --> 00:22:23.600
compared to the
N1, so we believe

00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:26.780
that is going to improve our
performance or consistency.

00:22:26.780 --> 00:22:30.860
And using smaller core
counts on a given socket

00:22:30.860 --> 00:22:33.470
means you have less probability
of having a neighbor.

00:22:33.470 --> 00:22:34.868
So it's pretty great.

00:22:34.868 --> 00:22:35.660
Back to the slides.

00:22:39.870 --> 00:22:43.070
All right, let's do it live.

00:22:43.070 --> 00:22:43.830
I will do it live.

00:22:43.830 --> 00:22:46.380
Like I said, I'll do it live
at the end if we have time.

00:22:46.380 --> 00:22:47.510
It takes 30 minutes or so.

00:22:50.550 --> 00:22:53.730
Memory-- there's a bunch
of great memory tests.

00:22:53.730 --> 00:22:57.120
Anyone who's investigated this
finds a bazillion of them.

00:22:57.120 --> 00:22:59.880
Multichase, Lmbench is great.

00:22:59.880 --> 00:23:04.920
Stream is part of HPCC and
is one of the granddaddies

00:23:04.920 --> 00:23:06.690
of memory performance tests.

00:23:06.690 --> 00:23:08.730
And there's just a
vast array of these.

00:23:08.730 --> 00:23:11.580
We'll be using multichase here
because developed at Google,

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:14.130
and I have to give
my friends props.

00:23:14.130 --> 00:23:16.286
So we'll try multichase.

00:23:16.286 --> 00:23:21.210
It's generally not as useful
as a stack or compute,

00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:22.920
but it can show a few issues.

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:24.780
It can show the NUMA
support of the VMs.

00:23:24.780 --> 00:23:26.550
Is it perfect or not?

00:23:26.550 --> 00:23:29.310
Are there NUMA
issues underlying?

00:23:29.310 --> 00:23:31.440
If you look at the
graph correctly,

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:33.090
and you squint, and
you turn your head,

00:23:33.090 --> 00:23:36.420
you can figure out if there's
host, huge page, or giant

00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:39.840
or large page support,
like 1 gig or 2 meg pages.

00:23:39.840 --> 00:23:42.182
If you really look, you can
probably figure this out.

00:23:42.182 --> 00:23:44.640
And so you can also figure out
if there's some weird kernel

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:50.238
KVM host memory manager issues
where locking comes into play.

00:23:50.238 --> 00:23:52.530
Because once in a while when
you do a memory benchmark,

00:23:52.530 --> 00:23:54.930
you can see some
interesting effect.

00:23:54.930 --> 00:23:57.600
And you can figure out if,
way underneath the hood,

00:23:57.600 --> 00:23:59.220
there is some kind
of kernel locking

00:23:59.220 --> 00:24:01.110
bug that is causing you issues.

00:24:01.110 --> 00:24:03.420
We've got a lot of mileage
out of running these tests

00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:05.850
over the years, and we
found a number of bugs

00:24:05.850 --> 00:24:10.150
throughout our stack
and perhaps in others.

00:24:10.150 --> 00:24:11.660
Again, a little bit faint--

00:24:11.660 --> 00:24:13.990
I have minimal
control of my staff.

00:24:13.990 --> 00:24:15.896
The dark green is the C2.

00:24:15.896 --> 00:24:18.827
The light green is the N1.

00:24:18.827 --> 00:24:20.410
And this is the
latency of multichase.

00:24:20.410 --> 00:24:23.680
So those are in nanoseconds
along the horizontal.

00:24:23.680 --> 00:24:27.430
Vertical, of course, as always
in a CDF, is percentile.

00:24:27.430 --> 00:24:30.880
What can I say other than
an enormous improvement?

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:33.460
We are talking
about big numbers.

00:24:33.460 --> 00:24:39.160
The P50 was about 142
nanoseconds with the N1.

00:24:39.160 --> 00:24:41.900
And with the C2, we're
talking about 87 nanoseconds,

00:24:41.900 --> 00:24:43.420
which just is about
speed of light

00:24:43.420 --> 00:24:46.780
given how virtualization works--

00:24:46.780 --> 00:24:48.140
today at least.

00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:50.980
And the real improvement
is at the tail.

00:24:50.980 --> 00:24:53.530
We had some real tail
issues with the N1

00:24:53.530 --> 00:24:55.820
on memory benchmarks.

00:24:55.820 --> 00:25:00.010
This was weirdly-- the tails
were not due to memory itself.

00:25:00.010 --> 00:25:02.440
They were due to,
interestingly, threads.

00:25:02.440 --> 00:25:05.290
So what would happen is you
have a VCPU thread, which

00:25:05.290 --> 00:25:06.250
is what backs--

00:25:06.250 --> 00:25:08.470
what you think of as
the CPU within your VM.

00:25:08.470 --> 00:25:09.880
And once in a while--

00:25:09.880 --> 00:25:12.130
because it's really at the
99-point-something up here.

00:25:12.130 --> 00:25:14.120
It's really far up.

00:25:14.120 --> 00:25:16.630
A user space thread or a kernel
thread would pre-empt you.

00:25:16.630 --> 00:25:18.910
And this particular
benchmark is very sensitive.

00:25:18.910 --> 00:25:20.650
And it would detect
that, and it would

00:25:20.650 --> 00:25:22.865
think that was an incredibly
long memory latency--

00:25:22.865 --> 00:25:24.818
a fetch, more precisely--

00:25:24.818 --> 00:25:26.610
while instead, it was
just a context switch

00:25:26.610 --> 00:25:27.580
underneath the hood.

00:25:27.580 --> 00:25:29.560
So with the software
with the C2,

00:25:29.560 --> 00:25:32.860
we have been pretty rigorous
in making sure, number one,

00:25:32.860 --> 00:25:37.600
every user space thread cannot
interfere with a VCPU thread.

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:40.090
Because there were some that
were interfering periodically.

00:25:40.090 --> 00:25:42.090
And furthermore, we have
been working diligently

00:25:42.090 --> 00:25:45.970
to move every possible
kernel thread out of the way

00:25:45.970 --> 00:25:49.760
and sequester them
away from VCPUs.

00:25:49.760 --> 00:25:51.090
That's an ongoing battle.

00:25:51.090 --> 00:25:53.050
There will always be a
couple of kernel threads

00:25:53.050 --> 00:25:54.610
that can mess you up.

00:25:54.610 --> 00:25:56.308
At the P99 point--

00:25:56.308 --> 00:25:57.850
I'm going to have
to get this right--

00:25:57.850 --> 00:26:02.050
99999 is where
we're shooting for.

00:26:02.050 --> 00:26:04.412
So we're going for
seven 9's there.

00:26:04.412 --> 00:26:06.370
There will always be a
couple of kernel threads

00:26:06.370 --> 00:26:09.110
that can mess you up unless
you do drastic changes.

00:26:09.110 --> 00:26:11.950
But we think we have line
of sight to seven 9's.

00:26:11.950 --> 00:26:14.220
We're not there yet,
but we're pretty close.

00:26:14.220 --> 00:26:17.260
So anyway-- and the other part
of the curve here where we had

00:26:17.260 --> 00:26:19.910
problems in the N1 is-- you can
see it's slanted pretty early,

00:26:19.910 --> 00:26:21.460
not just the extreme tails--

00:26:21.460 --> 00:26:22.660
was NUMA support.

00:26:22.660 --> 00:26:25.300
We didn't have good NUMA
support with the N1.

00:26:25.300 --> 00:26:27.420
With the N2, the NUMA
support is perfect.

00:26:27.420 --> 00:26:28.390
It's not too hard.

00:26:28.390 --> 00:26:29.710
We did it right this time.

00:26:29.710 --> 00:26:31.690
And it's perfect.

00:26:31.690 --> 00:26:34.600
So if memory latency
is crucial to your app,

00:26:34.600 --> 00:26:38.150
likely you will be buying a C2.

00:26:38.150 --> 00:26:39.150
Demo-- let's do it live.

00:26:39.150 --> 00:26:40.180
I'm just going to
show the graph again,

00:26:40.180 --> 00:26:42.760
because taking three minutes
probably is not right.

00:26:45.490 --> 00:26:47.648
Right on, so if I
just run this-- again,

00:26:47.648 --> 00:26:49.690
at the end, if I have an
extra couple of minutes,

00:26:49.690 --> 00:26:51.610
we'll run these.

00:26:51.610 --> 00:26:53.710
And you can see that
this is exactly the same

00:26:53.710 --> 00:26:55.395
as the graph we just ran.

00:26:55.395 --> 00:26:56.770
Interestingly--
I should tell you

00:26:56.770 --> 00:26:58.570
about how much
these cost to run.

00:26:58.570 --> 00:26:59.987
Like I said, I've
been telling you

00:26:59.987 --> 00:27:02.840
before it's cheap
to run these tests.

00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:05.290
These tests take on the order
of three to four minutes.

00:27:05.290 --> 00:27:11.420
This is done with 20 VMs
times 2, so 20 C2s, 20 N1s.

00:27:11.420 --> 00:27:14.350
A C2 or an N1 takes costs,
at the smaller sizes,

00:27:14.350 --> 00:27:16.960
about $0.10 each.

00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:20.240
They're running for on the
order of 1/20 of an hour.

00:27:20.240 --> 00:27:21.900
So that's $0.10 per hour.

00:27:21.900 --> 00:27:24.880
Multiplied by 40 VMs,
that costs you $0.20.

00:27:24.880 --> 00:27:27.412
So it's not overly expensive
to generate this graph.

00:27:27.412 --> 00:27:29.620
You can even, if you want,
get a little more fidelity

00:27:29.620 --> 00:27:30.930
by cranking up the
number of instances.

00:27:30.930 --> 00:27:32.388
If you want the
nice, smooth graphs

00:27:32.388 --> 00:27:36.010
we had earlier to 100 or 200,
then it's going to cost you $1.

00:27:36.010 --> 00:27:38.920
So these aren't horribly
hard to create on your own,

00:27:38.920 --> 00:27:42.465
and it's probably
a good investment.

00:27:42.465 --> 00:27:42.965
Back.

00:27:45.830 --> 00:27:47.330
All right.

00:27:47.330 --> 00:27:49.910
Networking-- oh, we're
blowing through the slides.

00:27:49.910 --> 00:27:52.820
I might have time to run
a demo if I have power.

00:27:52.820 --> 00:27:53.810
All right.

00:27:53.810 --> 00:27:59.030
There are a vast array
of networking tasks.

00:27:59.030 --> 00:28:02.720
There is TTCP, and it
has a really long set

00:28:02.720 --> 00:28:05.840
of descendants that
have forked and branched

00:28:05.840 --> 00:28:08.720
and slightly changed TTCP.

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:09.800
There's iPerf.

00:28:09.800 --> 00:28:11.180
Everyone's used iPerf.

00:28:11.180 --> 00:28:13.580
It's pretty much a classic.

00:28:13.580 --> 00:28:16.947
And it gives very
simple results.

00:28:16.947 --> 00:28:17.780
And there's NetPerf.

00:28:17.780 --> 00:28:21.200
NetPerf is complicated,
but the author

00:28:21.200 --> 00:28:23.648
is pretty cognizant of
just about everything

00:28:23.648 --> 00:28:24.690
going on with networking.

00:28:24.690 --> 00:28:27.332
This is why it has on the
order of 1,000 switches.

00:28:27.332 --> 00:28:28.290
And he works at Google.

00:28:28.290 --> 00:28:28.940
He's a friend of mine.

00:28:28.940 --> 00:28:31.250
So I'm going to use NetPerf
in the following examples.

00:28:31.250 --> 00:28:32.660
But it's a great test.

00:28:32.660 --> 00:28:35.660
It's just a little
difficult to run considering

00:28:35.660 --> 00:28:37.820
the number of flags.

00:28:37.820 --> 00:28:40.190
Again PKB will hide
all the flags for you

00:28:40.190 --> 00:28:42.200
and just output a
bunch of samples

00:28:42.200 --> 00:28:43.940
that you can throw
through some R scripts

00:28:43.940 --> 00:28:46.070
or whatever your
favorite visualizer is.

00:28:46.070 --> 00:28:49.190
15 minutes left on my laptop.

00:28:49.190 --> 00:28:51.320
And you can then see a CDF.

00:28:51.320 --> 00:28:53.067
So networking is
super complicated.

00:28:53.067 --> 00:28:55.400
These are snags, they call
them, but just complications.

00:28:55.400 --> 00:28:57.220
There's internal
versus external IPs.

00:28:57.220 --> 00:28:58.970
Very different performance
characteristics

00:28:58.970 --> 00:29:01.533
because the routing is
completely different.

00:29:01.533 --> 00:29:02.450
There's rate limiters.

00:29:02.450 --> 00:29:05.240
Some clouds have rate
limiters on their networking,

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:07.610
and that completely
changes everything

00:29:07.610 --> 00:29:09.710
about how you measure
networking if you

00:29:09.710 --> 00:29:13.340
want to see where their rate
limiters work or they kick in.

00:29:13.340 --> 00:29:15.560
Distance-- we'll be going with--

00:29:15.560 --> 00:29:18.320
distance matters both
at the short scale

00:29:18.320 --> 00:29:19.650
within a data center.

00:29:19.650 --> 00:29:22.400
So if you can do placement of
machines next to each other,

00:29:22.400 --> 00:29:24.230
that actually saves
you a little bit

00:29:24.230 --> 00:29:26.350
of time because of the
switches involved, not

00:29:26.350 --> 00:29:27.380
due to speed of light.

00:29:27.380 --> 00:29:31.490
But also, distance matters then
for how far your data centers

00:29:31.490 --> 00:29:33.170
are apart from each
other, because that

00:29:33.170 --> 00:29:35.300
will impact replication speed.

00:29:35.300 --> 00:29:37.700
And of course, speed of
light matters, and fiber

00:29:37.700 --> 00:29:41.580
matters a lot for when
you go across continents.

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:43.190
Oh, and I went
back a second here.

00:29:43.190 --> 00:29:45.440
I forgot fabrics vary.

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:50.030
We have built GCE over
the last five years.

00:29:50.030 --> 00:29:52.520
Over the five years, we
have revved our fabrics time

00:29:52.520 --> 00:29:53.480
and time again.

00:29:53.480 --> 00:29:56.330
Some of our older zones have
older fabrics which we then

00:29:56.330 --> 00:29:58.700
rev, but it takes time.

00:29:58.700 --> 00:30:00.350
So fabrics vary.

00:30:00.350 --> 00:30:04.490
And that changes-- that
makes TCP times or latency

00:30:04.490 --> 00:30:06.390
vary dramatically.

00:30:06.390 --> 00:30:08.270
And finally,
utilization-- utilization

00:30:08.270 --> 00:30:10.790
varies on a per-zone basis
because zones are big

00:30:10.790 --> 00:30:11.720
and/or small.

00:30:11.720 --> 00:30:15.500
And just for some
reason, VMs in some areas

00:30:15.500 --> 00:30:17.660
are much more heavily
utilized in others

00:30:17.660 --> 00:30:19.350
in terms of networking.

00:30:19.350 --> 00:30:22.463
And so you get a slightly
more noisy neighbor effect.

00:30:22.463 --> 00:30:23.880
All right, what
does this all look

00:30:23.880 --> 00:30:25.470
like with all those caveats?

00:30:25.470 --> 00:30:28.830
This is our
intra-zone networking.

00:30:28.830 --> 00:30:32.430
As you can see, this
is one line per zone,

00:30:32.430 --> 00:30:34.450
and there's a good
amount of difference,

00:30:34.450 --> 00:30:36.030
especially the tails
of those zones.

00:30:36.030 --> 00:30:38.910
That's normally due to
utilization effects.

00:30:38.910 --> 00:30:40.890
But there's also
differences at the P50.

00:30:40.890 --> 00:30:43.920
And most of those are
caused by utilization

00:30:43.920 --> 00:30:45.420
and by different fabrics.

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:47.160
There's, like,
two-- if you squint,

00:30:47.160 --> 00:30:49.770
you can see two giant
bands in the middle there.

00:30:49.770 --> 00:30:52.470
And that basically represents
a couple different generations

00:30:52.470 --> 00:30:56.300
of network fabric that we use.

00:30:56.300 --> 00:31:00.550
We are upgrading all
of our zones commonly.

00:31:00.550 --> 00:31:04.300
And the worst ones on here,
if I guess I can say it,

00:31:04.300 --> 00:31:05.410
happen to be in Asia 1.

00:31:05.410 --> 00:31:06.550
We're upgrading those.

00:31:06.550 --> 00:31:09.690
And we assume they will move
slightly more to the left.

00:31:09.690 --> 00:31:10.360
Again, sorry.

00:31:10.360 --> 00:31:12.277
I should have said what
kind of graph this is.

00:31:12.277 --> 00:31:13.840
I hope this is clear by now.

00:31:13.840 --> 00:31:16.780
It's a CDF, percent vertical.

00:31:16.780 --> 00:31:20.240
Horizontal-- unit of interest,
in this case microseconds.

00:31:20.240 --> 00:31:23.230
And P50-- I should
read the slides.

00:31:23.230 --> 00:31:24.340
I had notes here.

00:31:24.340 --> 00:31:26.470
We're talking-- this
is P50 in most modern--

00:31:26.470 --> 00:31:28.970
at P50 we're talking
50 microseconds,

00:31:28.970 --> 00:31:34.540
which is pretty reasonable for
ping pong latency, or TCPRR,

00:31:34.540 --> 00:31:37.360
as this test is called.

00:31:37.360 --> 00:31:37.860
All right.

00:31:37.860 --> 00:31:39.450
This is going to be
really hard to see.

00:31:39.450 --> 00:31:39.992
I don't know.

00:31:39.992 --> 00:31:42.550
I might have to walk down there.

00:31:42.550 --> 00:31:45.780
There's a little red line here
if you have your glasses on.

00:31:45.780 --> 00:31:47.850
I didn't realize how
thin this would be.

00:31:47.850 --> 00:31:50.610
Down here for the C2,
it's a little bit faster

00:31:50.610 --> 00:31:53.080
than everything we have today.

00:31:53.080 --> 00:31:55.060
This is due to a
couple of reasons.

00:31:55.060 --> 00:31:55.860
I don't know if you
all can see that,

00:31:55.860 --> 00:31:56.985
but it's a little bit over.

00:31:56.985 --> 00:31:58.590
It's at 38
microseconds as opposed

00:31:58.590 --> 00:32:02.430
to the rest of our networking.

00:32:02.430 --> 00:32:05.040
This is-- why is it
a little bit faster?

00:32:05.040 --> 00:32:06.040
A couple of reasons.

00:32:06.040 --> 00:32:09.350
One-- obviously, the faster
frequency of the processor

00:32:09.350 --> 00:32:12.080
allows it to really blow
through the TCP stack, which

00:32:12.080 --> 00:32:15.320
is one of the main
components of latency

00:32:15.320 --> 00:32:17.510
when it comes to running TCPRR.

00:32:17.510 --> 00:32:21.110
Obviously, if you use MPI and
some fantastic MPI library

00:32:21.110 --> 00:32:26.360
that may or may not be released
soon, then that all goes away,

00:32:26.360 --> 00:32:29.798
and this time
reduces dramatically.

00:32:29.798 --> 00:32:31.340
But with TCP, you
need your processor

00:32:31.340 --> 00:32:34.640
to power through that very
complicated TCP receive

00:32:34.640 --> 00:32:37.010
function that you can go
take a look in the kernel.

00:32:37.010 --> 00:32:39.662
It's hilarious.

00:32:39.662 --> 00:32:41.120
Anyway, one of the
things that this

00:32:41.120 --> 00:32:42.287
is using-- placement groups.

00:32:42.287 --> 00:32:47.050
Placement groups-- or rather,
by happenstance, the C2

00:32:47.050 --> 00:32:50.450
was in very low quantity when
we created all these graphs.

00:32:50.450 --> 00:32:53.960
And the machines were put very
close to each other physically.

00:32:53.960 --> 00:32:59.180
Normally our default cluster
manager configuration is

00:32:59.180 --> 00:33:03.380
to spread wide across the entire
cell to make its-- or zone,

00:33:03.380 --> 00:33:05.810
sorry in GC parlance--

00:33:05.810 --> 00:33:08.090
across the zone to make a
sea of higher reliability.

00:33:08.090 --> 00:33:09.590
So you have-- your
null 1 failure

00:33:09.590 --> 00:33:12.402
domain will hit two computers.

00:33:12.402 --> 00:33:14.360
So this is a little bit
better than it probably

00:33:14.360 --> 00:33:19.780
will be for a
computer in real life,

00:33:19.780 --> 00:33:21.350
but we think it'll
be pretty good.

00:33:21.350 --> 00:33:23.850
We think that we will have
a pretty good product here.

00:33:23.850 --> 00:33:27.450
And this will have pretty
solid frequencies--

00:33:27.450 --> 00:33:29.780
or rather, TCPRR latencies.

00:33:29.780 --> 00:33:30.520
All right.

00:33:30.520 --> 00:33:32.110
Between zones,
what can we learn?

00:33:32.110 --> 00:33:33.850
Like I said, you can just
run the script again.

00:33:33.850 --> 00:33:35.260
There will be a script for
this, and you can generate

00:33:35.260 --> 00:33:36.550
these very pretty bands.

00:33:36.550 --> 00:33:39.550
You can see, again,
there are two big clumps

00:33:39.550 --> 00:33:42.190
for how our clusters
work depending

00:33:42.190 --> 00:33:43.330
upon, basically, fabric.

00:33:43.330 --> 00:33:45.980
And we have a
policy on distance.

00:33:45.980 --> 00:33:50.500
So you're talking
basically 220 microseconds

00:33:50.500 --> 00:33:53.620
or 270 microseconds when
you're wanting to go

00:33:53.620 --> 00:33:57.070
between zones in GCE over TCP.

00:33:57.070 --> 00:33:59.440
It's moderately fast.

00:33:59.440 --> 00:34:01.318
Those are our largest
zones and regions.

00:34:01.318 --> 00:34:03.610
And then there's the fringe
zones that are a little bit

00:34:03.610 --> 00:34:07.485
faster to the right and a
little slower to the left.

00:34:07.485 --> 00:34:09.610
The little ones that are
closer to the right, those

00:34:09.610 --> 00:34:13.360
are almost always caused by
somewhat considerably smaller

00:34:13.360 --> 00:34:14.170
zones.

00:34:14.170 --> 00:34:16.719
We have a few zones
like the Montreal zone

00:34:16.719 --> 00:34:19.360
that was on an earlier
slide that are very

00:34:19.360 --> 00:34:21.889
close to each other physically.

00:34:21.889 --> 00:34:23.889
And so they have
incredibly better times.

00:34:23.889 --> 00:34:26.382
The times are substantially
better for those.

00:34:26.382 --> 00:34:28.090
And again, going all
the way to the left,

00:34:28.090 --> 00:34:30.469
there were a few
zones that have tails.

00:34:30.469 --> 00:34:32.409
And those are un-upgraded zones.

00:34:32.409 --> 00:34:37.409
As I said, Asia is
one of those areas.

00:34:37.409 --> 00:34:42.659
Speed of light-- very important
for many applications is

00:34:42.659 --> 00:34:45.900
transiting from-- this graph
is really hard to read--

00:34:45.900 --> 00:34:47.710
from one GO to another.

00:34:47.710 --> 00:34:49.560
And this is all of
our interconnects

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:53.040
between every zone in GCE.

00:34:53.040 --> 00:34:54.688
And this is almost entirely--

00:34:54.688 --> 00:34:56.730
it's not that interesting
of a slide in that it's

00:34:56.730 --> 00:35:03.355
almost entirely generated
from speed of light and fiber

00:35:03.355 --> 00:35:04.980
plus a couple of--
how many hops if you

00:35:04.980 --> 00:35:08.640
are a little bit
inconsistent in your routing.

00:35:08.640 --> 00:35:12.345
But generally, it follows
those numbers almost exactly.

00:35:12.345 --> 00:35:14.970
One interesting thing from this
graph is if you can take a look

00:35:14.970 --> 00:35:16.800
very carefully--
it's really hard--

00:35:16.800 --> 00:35:21.840
about 3/4 of the way up, we
see a slight change in the CDFs

00:35:21.840 --> 00:35:23.340
as you--

00:35:23.340 --> 00:35:25.810
around 3/4 for a
number of the lines.

00:35:25.810 --> 00:35:27.780
This was caused by
a routing glitch

00:35:27.780 --> 00:35:30.900
we had in early February--

00:35:30.900 --> 00:35:32.940
or last week of February, sorry.

00:35:32.940 --> 00:35:33.750
And it got caught.

00:35:33.750 --> 00:35:35.690
You too can run these tests--

00:35:35.690 --> 00:35:37.710
they're, again,
incredibly inexpensive--

00:35:37.710 --> 00:35:42.390
to monitor your cloud and
find out if you lost six

00:35:42.390 --> 00:35:45.960
milliseconds-- these are the
milliseconds, by the way,

00:35:45.960 --> 00:35:46.740
for your routing--

00:35:46.740 --> 00:35:48.790
because of a small
routing glitch.

00:35:48.790 --> 00:35:51.170
Well, my battery is dead.

00:35:51.170 --> 00:35:54.390
Finally-- I think this is
the final slide for data--

00:35:54.390 --> 00:35:56.170
is the networking throughput.

00:35:56.170 --> 00:35:58.430
Networking throughput
for GCE is pretty--

00:35:58.430 --> 00:36:01.930
compared to other clouds, or
in general, is pretty generous.

00:36:01.930 --> 00:36:05.060
We give 2 gigabits
per second per core.

00:36:05.060 --> 00:36:09.080
And that is not throttled
in some kind of mechanism.

00:36:09.080 --> 00:36:11.840
So that's a lot of--

00:36:11.840 --> 00:36:16.150
it's a lot of bandwidth to
give on a per-core basis.

00:36:16.150 --> 00:36:17.630
This caps out today.

00:36:17.630 --> 00:36:20.260
I don't member our
docs, but I think

00:36:20.260 --> 00:36:22.870
I'm safe to say it
caps out at 8 cores.

00:36:22.870 --> 00:36:24.930
So 16 gigabits is
where we cap out.

00:36:24.930 --> 00:36:29.530
There is a lot of work ongoing
to increase that number.

00:36:29.530 --> 00:36:32.643
And in the subsequent
weeks, months,

00:36:32.643 --> 00:36:34.060
you should be
seeing announcements

00:36:34.060 --> 00:36:37.600
that we have gone considerably
higher on a per-core basis,

00:36:37.600 --> 00:36:38.738
so we increased the caps.

00:36:38.738 --> 00:36:40.030
But this is where we are today.

00:36:40.030 --> 00:36:43.720
And this is the CDF yet
again, percentile vertical.

00:36:43.720 --> 00:36:46.270
Horizontal is throughput.

00:36:46.270 --> 00:36:48.390
And you can see most of
the time you will get--

00:36:48.390 --> 00:36:50.390
on your n1-standard
[INAUDIBLE],, you will get--

00:36:50.390 --> 00:36:52.210
and each line is a zone--

00:36:52.210 --> 00:36:54.800
you will be getting your
16 gigabits a second.

00:36:54.800 --> 00:36:56.420
Once in a while,
it will drop down.

00:36:56.420 --> 00:36:59.440
Some zones drop down more
commonly than others.

00:36:59.440 --> 00:37:01.190
Again, older
fabrics generally is

00:37:01.190 --> 00:37:04.040
where you'll see more of
the issues on the tails.

00:37:08.970 --> 00:37:10.280
All right.

00:37:10.280 --> 00:37:12.020
Next.

00:37:12.020 --> 00:37:13.660
And we can try the demo.

00:37:13.660 --> 00:37:14.160
Let's see.

00:37:14.160 --> 00:37:16.716
I'm almost at my last
dregs here of power.

00:37:20.420 --> 00:37:25.160
And two CDFs-- the C2
and an n1-standard-4.

00:37:25.160 --> 00:37:28.610
I don't remember which zone
the n1-standard-4 was in.

00:37:28.610 --> 00:37:32.420
You can see the tails
are fantastic on the C2.

00:37:32.420 --> 00:37:35.280
This is, again, almost
certainly an artifact,

00:37:35.280 --> 00:37:38.030
number one of the high
frequency of the C2,

00:37:38.030 --> 00:37:42.870
and secondarily of them being
in close physical proximity.

00:37:42.870 --> 00:37:44.900
This is essentially
with placement groups.

00:37:44.900 --> 00:37:46.850
Even though it wasn't
a placement group API,

00:37:46.850 --> 00:37:50.005
it was that they
happened to be there

00:37:50.005 --> 00:37:52.130
because we had very limited
quantities at the time.

00:37:52.130 --> 00:37:57.530
But the numbers are fantastic,
38 microseconds, almost a P99,

00:37:57.530 --> 00:37:59.450
almost no tails.

00:37:59.450 --> 00:38:02.030
We hope that we can deliver
this relatively soon

00:38:02.030 --> 00:38:03.590
in large clusters.

00:38:03.590 --> 00:38:04.790
Back to the slides.

00:38:10.330 --> 00:38:11.820
All right.

00:38:11.820 --> 00:38:13.290
Oh, I forgot there's more.

00:38:13.290 --> 00:38:15.660
Block devices--
remote block devices

00:38:15.660 --> 00:38:18.340
are just like networking in
that they're complicated.

00:38:18.340 --> 00:38:19.980
They have all
networking problems,

00:38:19.980 --> 00:38:22.140
plus they have media problems.

00:38:22.140 --> 00:38:26.130
They are very complicated
to benchmark correctly.

00:38:26.130 --> 00:38:27.300
There are many SKUs.

00:38:27.300 --> 00:38:30.180
Every cloud has a bunch
of different SKUs.

00:38:30.180 --> 00:38:32.850
Every zone is completely
different due to fabric

00:38:32.850 --> 00:38:35.273
being different, being due to
the generation of the media

00:38:35.273 --> 00:38:35.940
being different.

00:38:35.940 --> 00:38:37.023
Cloud is always like this.

00:38:37.023 --> 00:38:38.753
We're always buying
different media

00:38:38.753 --> 00:38:40.920
as the generations go along,
so there's a little bit

00:38:40.920 --> 00:38:43.170
of inconsistency there.

00:38:43.170 --> 00:38:47.040
Load-- the media almost
always, or quite commonly, get

00:38:47.040 --> 00:38:49.860
slammed, especially
for spinning rust.

00:38:49.860 --> 00:38:54.240
But even SSD get
slammed continually

00:38:54.240 --> 00:38:55.800
for these remote block devices.

00:38:55.800 --> 00:38:58.590
So they're all
very highly subbed.

00:38:58.590 --> 00:39:01.590
And so this causes a variance
in many different ways.

00:39:01.590 --> 00:39:04.690
And this all varies on a zone,
region, event time basis.

00:39:04.690 --> 00:39:07.770
So benchmarking, this
is moderately hard.

00:39:07.770 --> 00:39:10.050
Drive sizes modify
the performance,

00:39:10.050 --> 00:39:16.490
you'll see, in many complex ways
that are not easily intuited.

00:39:16.490 --> 00:39:18.980
Number one-- obviously, we
tell you on the web page,

00:39:18.980 --> 00:39:21.260
you'll get this many
IOPS per gigabyte,

00:39:21.260 --> 00:39:23.630
and everyone else has
a very similar model.

00:39:23.630 --> 00:39:26.900
But furthermore, it turns out
that the internal programming

00:39:26.900 --> 00:39:29.930
of these things means that
larger disks have a little bit

00:39:29.930 --> 00:39:33.380
more compute necessary to figure
out where to do the LDA to PBA

00:39:33.380 --> 00:39:34.522
translations.

00:39:34.522 --> 00:39:36.230
And that little bit
more compute makes it

00:39:36.230 --> 00:39:38.340
so that they're just
a little bit slower.

00:39:38.340 --> 00:39:40.250
So you have to try a
different drive sizes,

00:39:40.250 --> 00:39:42.083
and they all have
different characteristics.

00:39:42.083 --> 00:39:43.160
So it's just really hard.

00:39:43.160 --> 00:39:44.960
And throttling--
the throttling--

00:39:44.960 --> 00:39:49.010
because every cloud
has block devices

00:39:49.010 --> 00:39:52.390
that are subbed very hard--
they're very hard-- they're

00:39:52.390 --> 00:39:54.940
subbed to a very high degree.

00:39:54.940 --> 00:39:57.225
They have very complex
throttling mechanisms.

00:39:57.225 --> 00:39:59.350
So you have to understand
the throttling mechanisms

00:39:59.350 --> 00:40:02.820
that every cloud has
before you benchmark them.

00:40:02.820 --> 00:40:04.820
So anyway, that's all
this to say block devices,

00:40:04.820 --> 00:40:06.890
sadly, are pretty complicated.

00:40:06.890 --> 00:40:10.290
But we can just try random reads
because random reads are easy.

00:40:10.290 --> 00:40:12.230
So let's make a device
that's, on this size,

00:40:12.230 --> 00:40:15.200
500 gigabytes in size, throw
some random reads against it,

00:40:15.200 --> 00:40:17.430
and see what happens.

00:40:17.430 --> 00:40:18.290
This is pretty easy.

00:40:18.290 --> 00:40:20.150
And it turns out
for us, the bands

00:40:20.150 --> 00:40:21.670
are pretty tight, actually.

00:40:21.670 --> 00:40:25.275
They're on the order
of 600 microseconds.

00:40:25.275 --> 00:40:27.650
And interestingly-- you wouldn't
know this from external,

00:40:27.650 --> 00:40:30.440
but I can just say
that's about half

00:40:30.440 --> 00:40:34.010
and half in terms of
infrastructure time,

00:40:34.010 --> 00:40:36.080
in terms of
networking and media.

00:40:36.080 --> 00:40:37.520
And about half of
that is compute.

00:40:37.520 --> 00:40:39.560
There is-- like I was
saying, the computation,

00:40:39.560 --> 00:40:41.390
if you figure out where
to store the blocks

00:40:41.390 --> 00:40:44.160
and how to encode them,
is moderately complicated.

00:40:44.160 --> 00:40:46.160
And this is pretty common
across all the clouds,

00:40:46.160 --> 00:40:47.960
that that computation
is actually

00:40:47.960 --> 00:40:49.400
a large portion of the expense.

00:40:49.400 --> 00:40:51.980
And you'll see numbers
around 600 or slightly

00:40:51.980 --> 00:40:58.960
around there for most clouds for
4k random reads, the standard.

00:40:58.960 --> 00:41:02.347
Random writes-- a little more
random in terms of performance.

00:41:02.347 --> 00:41:04.180
You have a couple of
zones that are having--

00:41:04.180 --> 00:41:06.500
that obviously
where, in this case,

00:41:06.500 --> 00:41:08.350
the media is quite subbed.

00:41:08.350 --> 00:41:10.435
And so these are
SSD again where they

00:41:10.435 --> 00:41:11.560
are pushing the media hard.

00:41:11.560 --> 00:41:14.060
And the media is a little
bit older generation.

00:41:14.060 --> 00:41:17.470
But the vast majority is on
the order of 450 microseconds.

00:41:17.470 --> 00:41:21.210
You may be wondering, how is
a write faster than a read?

00:41:21.210 --> 00:41:23.310
Because I know how SSDs work.

00:41:23.310 --> 00:41:24.850
Writes are incredibly hard.

00:41:24.850 --> 00:41:28.050
It's because, just
like most drives,

00:41:28.050 --> 00:41:29.760
everyone has a little
bit of a cheat.

00:41:29.760 --> 00:41:34.050
We have battery-backed computers
that back all of our SSDs.

00:41:34.050 --> 00:41:37.350
So we triple write
to three computers

00:41:37.350 --> 00:41:38.670
that are battery-backed.

00:41:38.670 --> 00:41:41.040
And I was told by
the storage, ATL,

00:41:41.040 --> 00:41:44.340
that I actually battery
back at least three times.

00:41:44.340 --> 00:41:48.150
We are battery-backed, and
it goes to three machines.

00:41:48.150 --> 00:41:50.770
And then when
[INAUDIBLE] is achieved,

00:41:50.770 --> 00:41:52.640
then we let you continue.

00:41:52.640 --> 00:41:55.830
And then asynchronously,
it is flushed to the media.

00:41:58.720 --> 00:42:02.590
In summary, that was
a lot of data slides.

00:42:02.590 --> 00:42:03.550
I'm sorry.

00:42:03.550 --> 00:42:05.920
But this is what
they pay me for.

00:42:05.920 --> 00:42:08.230
Sorry to put you through that.

00:42:08.230 --> 00:42:11.328
Cloud is dynamic, and
boot times are really

00:42:11.328 --> 00:42:12.370
what power that dynamism.

00:42:12.370 --> 00:42:14.950
Please take advantage
of quick boot times.

00:42:14.950 --> 00:42:18.160
Use your computers only when
you need them and turn them off.

00:42:18.160 --> 00:42:20.080
You'll save lots of
money, and everyone wins.

00:42:20.080 --> 00:42:22.520
And you'll push us harder
and harder to be better.

00:42:22.520 --> 00:42:24.120
And I like that.

00:42:24.120 --> 00:42:26.770
IaaS is complex, but
to a first order,

00:42:26.770 --> 00:42:30.497
you can benchmark this pretty
easily and pretty cheaply.

00:42:30.497 --> 00:42:32.080
All of those graphs--
like our graphs,

00:42:32.080 --> 00:42:36.040
the ones I had that
were in light green--

00:42:36.040 --> 00:42:37.300
have more sample points.

00:42:37.300 --> 00:42:40.010
But they cost, at most, $100.

00:42:40.010 --> 00:42:42.700
And I probably went too crazy
on some of those graphs.

00:42:42.700 --> 00:42:44.890
You can make cheaper
graphs for a couple bucks,

00:42:44.890 --> 00:42:48.220
and you can get a good
idea of what's going on.

00:42:48.220 --> 00:42:50.890
The C2-- we're
pretty happy with it.

00:42:50.890 --> 00:42:52.990
So far in all of our
testing, we think

00:42:52.990 --> 00:42:56.020
this will be a fine machine.

00:42:56.020 --> 00:42:58.760
Please go buy them and make my
capacity planners really upset.

00:42:58.760 --> 00:43:00.930
They don't know how they're
going to buy enough so quickly.

00:43:00.930 --> 00:43:03.140
And I hope you all go out,
rush out and buy the C2.

00:43:03.140 --> 00:43:04.120
It's fantastic.

00:43:04.120 --> 00:43:06.193
And most importantly,
don't trust me.

00:43:06.193 --> 00:43:07.610
Don't trust anything
you saw here.

00:43:07.610 --> 00:43:08.800
These will be easy to run.

00:43:08.800 --> 00:43:10.290
Go to that location.

00:43:10.290 --> 00:43:13.260
There will be a directory
with demos in it really soon.

00:43:13.260 --> 00:43:15.610
I have people working
on finishing the code

00:43:15.610 --> 00:43:17.733
and getting it through
code review right now.

00:43:17.733 --> 00:43:19.900
So within a few days, there
should be a place there.

00:43:19.900 --> 00:43:22.270
And you can just press
up arrow, like I did,

00:43:22.270 --> 00:43:24.600
and get numbers, and
maybe believe them.

00:43:24.600 --> 00:43:25.980
Thank you.

00:43:25.980 --> 00:43:30.230
[APPLAUSE]

